<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Srimanta Sankardev</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1449">1449</a>–<a href="/years/1568">1568</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Sankaradeva, Sankardev, Sankardeva, Srimanta Sankaradeva</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Sankardev was an Assamese saint, scholar, playwright and social reformer. He founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ekasarana-dharma">Ekasarana Dharma</a>, a Vaishnava devotional movement centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> that rejected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> barriers and idol worship, and established monasteries called sattras. His plays, songs and the dance tradition that grew from them, now known as Sattriya, shaped Assamese culture. In later life he found protection at the court of the Koch king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara-narayan">Nara Narayan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/srimanta-sankardev.jpg" alt="Srimanta Sankardev" width="320" height="506" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jrkalita · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sankaradeva.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Sankardev was born as Sankaravara into the chief Baro-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuyan">Bhuyans</a> family at Bordowa in the present-day Nagaon district, in about 1449. The Bhuyans were independent landlords in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/assam">Assam</a>, and his family were Saktas. His father died of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smallpox">smallpox</a> when he was about seven, and his grandmother Khersuti raised him.</p><p>At twelve he entered the school of Mahendra Kandali, who gave him the name Sankardev, and he studied grammar and the Indian scriptures. After leaving school in his late teens he took up his duties as Shiromani Bhuyan. In 1481 he set out on a twelve-year pilgrimage to the major seats of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vaishnavism">Vaishnavism</a>, including Puri, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mathura">Mathura</a>, Dwaraka and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rameswaram">Rameswaram</a>, and composed his first borgeet in Brajavali at Badrikashram in 1488.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>On his return he refused to take back the Shiromaniship. He built a prayer house at Bordowa and received a copy of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavata-purana">Bhagavata Purana</a> from Jagadisa Mishra of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mithila">Mithila</a>, an event regarded as critical to the development of Ekasarana, which holds <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> to be the only God. Pressure from the Ahom and Koch kings drove the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhuyan">Bhuyans</a> from their lands, and Sankardev moved east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a>, to Gangmau and then to Dhuwahat, where he won his chief disciple Madhavdev after a debate. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmins">Brahmins</a> complained to the Ahom king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suhungmung">Suhungmung</a>, but Sankardev convinced the king that he was no threat to the social order.</p><p>He later settled in the Koch kingdom, at Patbausi near Barpeta. When Koch king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nara-narayan">Nara Narayan</a> ordered his arrest for spreading a new religion, the general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chilarai">Chilarai</a> persuaded the king to hear him instead. Sankardev refuted the charges at court and was given freedom to teach. At Chilarai's request he supervised the weaving of the Vrindavani vastra, a long tapestry of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a>'s life at Vrindavan; parts of it survive in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. He died at Bheladonga in Kochbehar in 1568, having declined the king's wish to be initiated.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Sankardev preached devotion to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> through hearing and singing his deeds, in the attitude of a servant of God, and did not worship Radha beside him. He initiated people of all <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">castes</a> and religions, including Muslims, and called on followers to give up Vedic rites. His Kirtana-ghosha, narrative verses for communal singing, remains in many Assamese homes.</p><p>He rendered eight books of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhagavata-purana">Bhagavata Purana</a> into Assamese, wrote one-act plays called Ankia Naat, and composed borgeets, of which 34 of 240 survive. He is credited with originating the Sattriya dance, now a classical dance form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>, and the sattras, the monasteries that carry his teaching on.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Sankardev” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Hinduism spread at court from the sixteenth century, while the Neo-Vaishnavite monasteries (satras) inspired by <b>Srimanta Sankardev</b> won wide popular followings.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ahom-kingdom">Ahom kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In the east Chaitanya Mahaprabhu took the vows of a renunciant in 1510 and spent his later years at Puri, while <b>Srimanta Sankardev</b> founded the Ekasarana Dharma in Assam.</blockquote><a href="/movement/bhakti-movement">Bhakti movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>Nara Narayan rebuilt the Kamakhya temple and patronised the Vaishnava saint <b>Sankardev</b> and Assamese and Sanskrit literature.</blockquote><a href="/state/koch-kingdom">Koch Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
